     "result_text_tail": "ed at the CEO and executive team deciding at January 2027 planning.\n- **Allowance:** about 667 requests per seat per month, which keeps AI cost at half the $12 premium. This is my rule of thumb, not something in the pack; adjust it if you prefer.\n- **Fallback option:** I kept \"mobile app first, Copilot later\" as the fallback. The pack doesn't say how long the mobile rewrite takes, so that option has no launch date.\n\n**Things to know**\n- **Grades:** all 133 numbers trace to the briefing pack or a calculation from it, and no placeholders remain. 107 are graded B (taken straight from the pack) and 26 are E (my estimates). Each estimate shows its working on the slide and in the workbook.\n- **Inconsistency in the pack:** the beta note says the top 10% of accounts made 58% of AI requests, but Table 2's figures imply about 40%. I used Table 2 for costs and footnoted this on page 20.\n- **Upper bounds:** the 14% take-up, the $640K win-back and the $1.1M mobile risk are all worst or best cases, and each slide says so.\n- **QA warnings:** check.py leaves two warnings, which mistake the \"Q2\"/\"Q3\" quarter labels in two titles for numbers.\n\nThe most useful next step is to get the Q4 2026 win rate against Routewise, after their discount ended. It's the cleanest test of whether AI is really losing deals.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- `brightwork-copilot.pptx`\n- `brightwork-copilot-sources.xlsx`\n- `brightwork-copilot.json` (the editable spec; the script that builds it is `./work/build.py`)"
    }
